  "id": "DrugBank_DB00352 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Tioguanine appears to be associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a pathway involving Idarubicin, a chemotherapy agent. The evidence suggests that Tioguanine may be used to treat a broad category of AML, including both inherited and non-inherited forms, with Idarubicin acting as a common therapeutic target. The presence of a T(9;11)(p22;q23) chromosomal translocation in AML is a specific subtype that is often treated with Idarubicin. The integration of these pathways implies that Tioguanine may function as a broad-spectrum treatment for AML, potentially through a mechanism that enhances Idarubicin's efficacy or supports its therapeutic action. The evidence supports a relationship where Tioguanine is linked to AML through a shared pathway involving Idarubicin, with the T(9;11)(p22;q23) subtype being a specific manifestation of the disease that is treated by Idarubicin. This suggests that Tioguanine may have a role in the treatment of AML with this specific chromosomal abnormality, though further research is needed to clarify the exact mechanism of action.",
